  "requirement": "A `{req}` definition MUST use an opening fence of at least three colons immediately followed by `{req} <name>`. A closing fence MUST have at least as many colons as the opening fence. The requirement body MUST be all Markdown between those fence lines, including headings, examples, diagrams, and blank lines, and MUST NOT include text after the closing fence. Leading and trailing whitespace MUST be excluded when comparing the body with evidence.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: the requirement is met.**\n\n**Implementation**\n- `colon_fence` (src/lib.rs) accepts a fence line only if it has at most 3 leading spaces and at least 3 colons. It returns the text after the colons and the fence length.\n- `definition_blocks` (src/lib.rs) requires the text immediately after the colons to start with `{req}` (or `{example}` / `{definition}`). That text must be followed by whitespace and a valid name. Otherwise the line is not treated as a definition.\n- The closing fence is the first later line that is not inside a Markdown code block and meets two conditions. Its colon count is at least the opening count (`count >= fence_length`), and nothing follows the colons (`suffix.trim().is_empty()`).\n  - The code-block exclusion means `:::` inside a fenced example does not end the span.\n  - Because only a bare colon line closes a block, text after a closing fence is not treated as part of that fence. The body runs from the end of the opening line (`body_start`) to the start of the closing line (`body_end`), so it holds all Markdown between the fences, including headings, code, diagrams and blank lines.\n- `collect_definitions` (src/evidence.rs, lines 261-262) takes `content[body_start..body_end].trim()`, which drops leading and trailing whitespace. A block with no closing fence is reported as an error (\"missing a closing colon fence\").\n- `replace_definitions` (src/lib.rs) uses the same block spans when rendering, so the book output and the evidence check agree.\n\n**Tests**\n- `colon_requirement_span_includes_markdown_and_excludes_following_text` (src/evidence.rs) uses a body containing a code fence with `:::` inside, an ATX heading, a setext heading and blank lines. It checks that the whole body is matched as the requirement text. It also checks that the \"Outside the requirement.\" text and the second requirement are excluded.\n- `a_longer_colon_fence_contains_a_shorter_nested_directive` (src/lib.rs) checks that a four-colon opening fence is closed only by a fence of at least four colons. A nested three-colon directive stays inside the body, and text after the closing fence is excluded.\n- `reports_a_missing_closing_colon_fence` (src/evidence.rs) checks the unclosed case.\n\n**Gaps**\n- I did not see a dedicated test for CRLF line endings or for the 0–3 leading-space tolerance. The code handles both (CR is trimmed from lines, and `colon_fence` allows up to 3 leading spaces), and these are minor edge cases.\n- No acknowledged FIXMEs.\n\nThe main design points are covered: fence length, nesting, code-fenced colons, trimming, exclusion of text after the fence, and the unclosed block.",
